//? Title: Diffusion Maps: From Three Points to Iris and a Swiss Roll
//? Subtitle: Inspecting normalization, bandwidth, spectral time and the limits of two-coordinate recovery
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Given high-dimensional observations, we want a few coordinates that preserve
how observations connect through nearby points. A global linear projection
can miss a curved surface, while inappropriate neighbourhood scales can join
unrelated parts of it. Diffusion maps builds coordinates from a reversible
random walk on a Gaussian similarity graph. Three points make every
normalization hand-checkable before we examine all 150 iris flowers and the
original 400-point Swiss roll. Actual kernel and transition matrices expose
the eigenvector scaling. Computed parameter comparisons retain the original
iris separation and Swiss roll angle-recovery experiments, alongside the
local-kernel failure to recover height in the first four coordinates. PCA and classical
MDS provide controls on exactly the same raw observations.
)

\toc

> A walk on three points

Take the one-dimensional observations x=(0,1,2), epsilon=1/log(2), alpha=0
and diffusion time tau=1. The Gaussian similarities of neighbouring points
are 1/2; the similarity between the endpoints is 1/16. Keep the unit
self-loops. Thus

\[
K=\begin{bmatrix}1&1/2&1/16\\1/2&1&1/2\\1/16&1/2&1\end{bmatrix},
\quad d=(25/16,2,25/16),\quad
P=\begin{bmatrix}16/25&8/25&1/25\\1/4&1/2&1/4\\1/25&8/25&16/25\end{bmatrix}.
\]

The rows of P sum to one. Its eigenvalues are 1, 3/5 and 9/50.
The stationary probabilities are (25,32,25)/82. The nonconstant
antisymmetric eigenvector distinguishes the two endpoints. Its normalization
matters: the library uses unit norm weighted by d, rather than by the
stationary probabilities pi=d/sum(d).

This complete program prints the matrices from the shared executing fit
kernel. It then checks the endpoint diffusion distance at four times against
the hand-derived formula D_tau(0,2)^2=(164/25)(3/5)^(2 tau). The other
nonconstant eigenvector has equal endpoint values and contributes zero for
this pair. The full three-point matrices remain visible even though the
model retains only one nontrivial coordinate.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/diffusion_three_points.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame d;d.add_column("x",std::vector<double>{0,1,2});
  const auto t=dm::stats::diffusion_maps_trace(d,{"x"},{1,1/std::log(2.0),0,1});
  std::print("| row | K0 | K1 | K2 | degree | P0 | P1 | P2 | stationary |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<3;++i)std::print("| {} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.6f} |\n",i,t.kernel(i,0),t.kernel(i,1),t.kernel(i,2),t.degrees[i],t.transition(i,0),t.transition(i,1),t.transition(i,2),t.stationary[i]);
  std::print("\nEigenvalues: {:.6f}, {:.6f}, {:.6f}.\n\n",t.eigenvalues[0],t.eigenvalues[1],t.eigenvalues[2]);
  std::print("| time | spectral endpoint distance squared | closed form | absolute error |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  double total=0;for(double degree:t.degrees)total+=degree;
  const double delta=t.right_eigenvectors(0,1)-t.right_eigenvectors(2,1);
  for(double time:{0.,1.,2.,8.}){
    const double actual=total*delta*delta*std::pow(t.eigenvalues[1],2*time),expected=164./25*std::pow(.6,2*time);
    if(std::abs(actual-expected)>1e-12)return 1;
    std::print("| {:.0f} | {:.8f} | {:.8f} | {:.2e} |\n",time,actual,expected,std::abs(actual-expected));
  }
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram(65,1100);dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle box;box.width=4;box.height=1.8;box.font_size=23;
  diagram.node("x",{0,0},"Raw observations\nx = 0, 1, 2",box)
    .node("k",{7,0},std::format("Gaussian similarities\nK01 = {:.4f}; K02 = {:.4f}",t.kernel(0,1),t.kernel(0,2)),box)
    .node("p",{7,-4},std::format("Row-normalized walk\nP00 = {:.2f}; P01 = {:.2f}",t.transition(0,0),t.transition(0,1)),box)
    .node("e",{0,-4},std::format("Right Markov eigenvectors\nRetain lambda = {:.2f}",t.eigenvalues[1]),box)
    .edge("x","k","squared distance / epsilon").edge("k","p","alpha = 0; divide by degree")
    .edge("p","e","symmetric eigenproblem")
    .text({3.5,2},"Three observations: local similarity becomes a diffusion coordinate",25)
    .text({3.5,-6},"S = D^(-1/2) K D^(-1/2); psi = D^(-1/2) v; coordinate = lambda^time psi",20)
    .save_svg("build/diffusion_three_points.svg");
}
```
