\caption(The actual three-point fit: Gaussian similarities become transition probabilities, then a right Markov eigenvector. The printed matrices and distance check supply the intermediate numbers.)
\alttext(Four boxes show raw points, Gaussian similarities, a row-normalized walk and the retained eigenvector; arrow labels describe each operation.)

> What the normalization does

For an n by p matrix of complete raw feature rows, form the dense squared
Euclidean distances and Gaussian kernel, including the diagonal:

\[
r_{ij}=\|x_i-x_j\|^2,\quad K_{ij}=\exp(-r_{ij}/\epsilon),\quad
q_i=\sum_jK_{ij},\quad A_{ij}=\frac{K_{ij}}{q_i^\alpha q_j^\alpha}.
\]

Next let d_i=sum_j A_ij, D=diag(d), P=D^(-1)A and
S=D^(-1/2) A D^(-1/2). A is symmetric, so S is symmetric and
P=D^(-1/2) S D^(1/2). If S v_l=lambda_l v_l and v_l has unit
Euclidean norm, then psi_l=D^(-1/2)v_l is a right eigenvector of P.
The library returns the columns lambda_l^tau psi_l for l=1,...,k,
discarding the leading constant mode. Using v directly, as in the original
report's displayed embedding formula, would omit the degree correction.

Reversibility follows immediately:
pi_i P_ij=A_ij/sum(d)=pi_j P_ji, with pi_i=d_i/sum(d).
The constant right eigenvector has eigenvalue one. The Gaussian Gram matrix
is positive semidefinite; the two diagonal congruences preserve that property,
so S has a nonnegative spectrum in exact arithmetic. Numerical negative
values within 128*n*machine_epsilon are clipped to zero; a more negative
value causes an error. Nonnegative real tau defines the spectral filter;
only integer tau is an ordinary count of discrete Markov steps. At tau=0,
every retained mode has multiplier one, including a numerically zero mode.

For integer tau, compare complete transition distributions using

\[
D_\tau(i,j)^2=\sum_r\frac{(P^\tau_{ir}-P^\tau_{jr})^2}{\pi_r}
=\left(\sum_r d_r\right)\sum_{\ell=1}^{n-1}
\lambda_\ell^{2\tau}(\psi_\ell(i)-\psi_\ell(j))^2.
\]

The second equality uses the orthonormal symmetric eigenbasis and similarity
transform. The factor sum(d) is essential under the library's D-weighted
normalization; multiplying its coordinates by sqrt(sum(d)) recovers the
stationary-normalized convention. Retaining only k modes omits nonnegative
terms, so the scaled embedded squared distance is a lower bound on this
full spectral distance, subject to roundoff. Sign flips cancel from squared
distances and absolute correlations. Rotations within a repeated eigenspace
also leave the full subspace's distances unchanged.

[Coifman and Lafon (2006)](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.acha.2006.04.006)
derive the diffusion construction and continuum limits. Alpha=0 uses kernel
degrees directly; alpha=1/2 gives a different density weighting; alpha=1
approaches a density-independent Laplace–Beltrami operator under the paper's
manifold, sampling and small-bandwidth conditions. Finite data at alpha=1
are not exactly density-free, nor must that choice maximize class separation.

Computing the dense distances costs O(n^2 p); the symmetric decomposition
costs O(n^3). Working storage is O(n^2+n p), while the ordinary fitted model
retains O(n k+n). The optional trace owns six n by n intermediate matrices
plus the embedding and row vectors. It defaults to a 1000-observation cap,
checked before dense allocation; zero disables only that cap. The ordinary
model retains its existing uncapped interface. This is an in-sample spectral
embedding with no optimizer, learned tensor weights or out-of-sample transform.

> Iris: inspect the observations before interpreting the embedding

The bundled iris data contain 150 flowers, four measurements in centimetres
and three species of 50 flowers each. Use all four measurements in their
original units. Species labels enter only after fitting, for colour and
external summaries. Petal length and width already show setosa separated
from the other two species.

The global bandwidth diagnostic is the median squared distance among all
11,175 unordered pairs. The original fit used epsilon=5.5, close to but
not equal to the measured median 5.57. We preserve that value. This program
also prints the eighth-neighbour scale; it does not automatically choose
epsilon from either statistic. With even counts the reusable scale summary
uses the upper middle sorted value, matching the original report rather than
averaging the two central distances.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/diffusion_iris.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");const auto iris=dm::datasets::iris();
  const std::vector<std::string> features{"Sepal.Length","Sepal.Width","Petal.Length","Petal.Width"};
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> columns;for(const auto& name:features)columns.push_back(iris.numeric_column(name).values());
  const auto x=dm::linalg::DenseMatrix<double>::from_columns(columns);
  const auto scales=dm::stats::manifold_distance_scales(x,8);
  const dm::stats::DiffusionMaps model(iris,features,{2,5.5,.5,1});
  std::vector<std::string> labels;for(auto i:model.kept_row_indices())labels.push_back(iris.string_at("Species",i));
  std::print("Rows: {}; numeric features: {}; complete rows: {}.\n\n",iris.nrows(),features.size(),model.observations());
  std::print("| squared-distance diagnostic (cm squared) | value |\n| :--- | ---: |\n| global upper median | {:.4f} |\n| global mean | {:.4f} |\n| eighth-neighbour upper median | {:.4f} |\n\n",scales.upper_median_squared,scales.mean_squared,scales.upper_median_neighbor_squared);
  std::print("Fit epsilon 5.5, alpha 0.5, time 1; eigenvalues {:.4f}, {:.4f}, {:.4f}.\n\n",model.eigenvalues()[0],model.eigenvalues()[1],model.eigenvalues()[2]);
  auto raw=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();const std::vector<dm::graphics::RGB> colors{{37,99,235},{220,38,38},{22,163,74}};
  std::size_t group=0;std::print("| species | rows |\n| :--- | ---: |\n");
  for(const auto& species:std::vector<std::string>{"setosa","versicolor","virginica"}){
    const auto subset=iris.filter([&](const dm::dstruct::DataFrame::Row& row){return row.get_string("Species")==species;});
    std::print("| {} | {} |\n",species,subset.nrows());
    raw.points(subset.numeric_column("Petal.Length").values(),subset.numeric_column("Petal.Width").values(),species,colors[group++],4);
  }
  raw.title("Raw petals: labels used for colour").x_label("petal length (cm)").y_label("petal width (cm)").y_limits(-.2,3.3);
  auto fitted=model.plot_embedding(labels);fitted.title("Iris: epsilon 5.5, alpha 0.5").x_label("diffusion coordinate 1").y_label("diffusion coordinate 2").y_limits(-.12,.16);
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(raw).add(fitted).size(1100,520).title("All 150 flowers; all four raw measurements enter the fit").fit_panels().save_svg("build/diffusion_iris.svg");
}
