> Bandwidth can overwhelm the alpha effect

For this sample the global squared-distance median is about 227.6, while
the median eighth-neighbour squared distance is about 11.46. The original
choices epsilon=227.6 and epsilon=5 differ by a factor of about 45.5.
At epsilon=227.6, a squared distance of 40 gives exp(-40/227.6), about 0.84;
at epsilon=5 it gives exp(-8), about 0.00034. A broad kernel therefore
connects across the roll's sheets much more strongly. The neighbour statistic
is a diagnostic reference, not a guarantee of a good neighbourhood scale.

Fit all six epsilon/alpha combinations once each with four retained modes.
The first table retains the original signed first-coordinate/angle and
second-coordinate/height correlations, and adds the sign-invariant angle
and arc-length magnitudes. The second table checks every one of the first
four coordinates against height. The figure shows absolute correlations
for comparisons that do not depend on arbitrary eigenvector sign.
The tests use the separate matrix-power definition of diffusion distance,
not just a second invocation of the fit, to check the normalization.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/diffusion_sweep.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto roll=dm::datasets::swiss_roll(400,0,42,dm::datasets::SwissRollEngine::Mt19937);
  const auto& t=roll.numeric_column("t").values();const auto& h=roll.numeric_column("height").values();const auto& arc=roll.numeric_column("arc_length").values();
  auto p=dm::graphics::RPlot::create(),q=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> height_rows;
  std::print("| epsilon | alpha | r(coord1,t) | r(coord2,height) | abs r(coord1,t) | abs r(coord1,arc length) |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::size_t series=0;for(double epsilon:{227.6,5.}){std::vector<double> angles,heights;
    for(double alpha:{0.,.5,1.}){
      const dm::stats::DiffusionMaps m(roll,{"x","y","z"},{4,epsilon,alpha,1});
      const double rt=dm::stats::pearson_correlation_test(m.dimension(0),t).estimate1,rh=dm::stats::pearson_correlation_test(m.dimension(1),h).estimate1;
      const double rs=dm::stats::pearson_correlation_test(m.dimension(0),arc).estimate1;
      angles.push_back(std::abs(rt));heights.push_back(std::abs(rh));
      std::print("| {:.1f} | {:.1f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} |\n",epsilon,alpha,rt,rh,std::abs(rt),std::abs(rs));
      std::vector<double> row{epsilon,alpha};for(std::size_t c=0;c<4;++c)row.push_back(std::abs(dm::stats::pearson_correlation_test(m.dimension(c),h).estimate1));height_rows.push_back(row);
    }
    const dm::graphics::RGB color=series++?dm::graphics::RGB{37,99,235}:dm::graphics::RGB{220,38,38};
    p.line({0,.5,1},angles,std::format("epsilon {:.1f}",epsilon),color).points({0,.5,1},angles,"",color,5);
    q.line({0,.5,1},heights,std::format("epsilon {:.1f}",epsilon),color).points({0,.5,1},heights,"",color,5);
  }
  std::print("\n| epsilon | alpha | height abs r, coord1 | coord2 | coord3 | coord4 |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(const auto& r:height_rows)std::print("| {:.1f} | {:.1f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} |\n",r[0],r[1],r[2],r[3],r[4],r[5]);
  p.title("First coordinate versus true angle").x_label("alpha").y_label("absolute Pearson correlation").x_ticks({0,.5,1},{"0","0.5","1"}).y_limits(0,1.23);
  q.title("Second coordinate versus true height").x_label("alpha").y_label("absolute Pearson correlation").x_ticks({0,.5,1},{"0","0.5","1"}).y_limits(0,1.23);
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(p).add(q).size(1100,520).title("Bandwidth comparison on exactly the same 400 observations").fit_panels().save_svg("build/diffusion_sweep.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| epsilon | alpha | r(coord1,t) | r(coord2,height) | abs r(coord1,t) | abs r(coord1,arc length) |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 227.6 | 0.0 | -0.1935 | -0.0438 | 0.1935 | 0.2599 |
| 227.6 | 0.5 | -0.2022 | -0.0286 | 0.2022 | 0.2712 |
| 227.6 | 1.0 | -0.2138 | -0.0075 | 0.2138 | 0.2877 |
| 5.0 | 0.0 | -0.9172 | -0.0946 | 0.9172 | 0.9562 |
| 5.0 | 0.5 | -0.9304 | -0.0607 | 0.9304 | 0.9663 |
| 5.0 | 1.0 | -0.9375 | -0.0271 | 0.9375 | 0.9715 |

| epsilon | alpha | height abs r, coord1 | coord2 | coord3 | coord4 |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 227.6 | 0.0 | 0.0753 | 0.0438 | 0.9953 | 0.0005 |
| 227.6 | 0.5 | 0.0774 | 0.0286 | 0.9958 | 0.0068 |
| 227.6 | 1.0 | 0.0788 | 0.0075 | 0.9962 | 0.0086 |
| 5.0 | 0.0 | 0.0019 | 0.0946 | 0.0666 | 0.2932 |
| 5.0 | 0.5 | 0.0031 | 0.0607 | 0.0441 | 0.3334 |
| 5.0 | 1.0 | 0.0014 | 0.0271 | 0.0175 | 0.3550 |
\image(figures/diffusion_sweep.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Parameter comparisons on the original roll. A local kernel improves the first coordinate’s angle correlation; the second coordinate still recovers little of the independent height.)
\alttext(Two line plots compare alpha zero, one half and one at epsilon 227.6 and five; the left measures angle correlation and the right height correlation.)

The broad kernel reaches only 0.1935–0.2138 absolute angle correlation
in its first coordinate. At epsilon=5, that becomes 0.9172, 0.9304 and
0.9375 for alpha zero, one half and one. Height shows a different tradeoff:
the broad kernel's third coordinate correlates 0.9953–0.9962 with height,
while every one of the local kernel's first four coordinates stays at or
below 0.3550. Poor height recovery is therefore specific to that local
embedding, rather than a universal inability to represent height.

> Compare with PCA and classical MDS on the same raw geometry

Keep the original selected diffusion fit epsilon=5, alpha=1, tau=1.
“Selected” here means the largest observed first-coordinate angle correlation
among the six inspected settings, using the same known coordinates for
evaluation. There is no independent selection or test set.

Centre PCA but disable its default scaling. Classical MDS uses Euclidean
distances from the same x,y,z values. For these Euclidean data, double
centring the squared distances gives X_centred X_centred^T. Its positive
principal coordinates therefore agree with unscaled PCA scores up to signs
and any rotations in repeated eigenspaces. They preserve ambient variance
rather than automatically unrolling the sheet.

The final table repeats the original signed correlations and reports the
best absolute angle correlation over the first two coordinates for each
method. Neighbour trustworthiness and continuity at k=8 add a different
question: how well are ambient nearest-neighbour ranks preserved? Those
scores refer to the original three-dimensional Euclidean geometry, which
can itself contain cross-sheet shortcuts. They do not measure exact
recovery of the known flat rectangle.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/diffusion_controls.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto roll=dm::datasets::swiss_roll(400,0,42,dm::datasets::SwissRollEngine::Mt19937);
  const std::vector<std::string> features{"x","y","z"};
  const dm::stats::DiffusionMaps diffusion(roll,features,{2,5,1,1});
  const dm::stats::PCA pca(roll,features,{true,false});const dm::stats::MDS mds(roll,features,{2,dm::stats::DistanceMetric::Euclidean});
  const auto& t=roll.numeric_column("t").values();const auto& h=roll.numeric_column("height").values();const auto colors=dm::graphics::value_colors(t);
  const auto input=dm::linalg::DenseMatrix<double>::from_columns({roll.numeric_column("x").values(),roll.numeric_column("y").values(),roll.numeric_column("z").values()});
  const auto pc=dm::linalg::DenseMatrix<double>::from_columns({pca.scores().col(0),pca.scores().col(1)});
  const std::vector<dm::linalg::DenseMatrix<double>> embeddings{diffusion.embedding(),pc,mds.embedding()};
  const std::vector<std::string> names{"Diffusion maps","PCA unscaled","Classical MDS"};
  std::print("| method | r(coord1,t) | r(coord2,height) | best abs r(t), first two | trustworthiness k8 | continuity k8 |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  auto layout=dm::graphics::RLayout::create(2,2);
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<embeddings.size();++i){const auto& e=embeddings[i];
    const double r0=dm::stats::pearson_correlation_test(e.col(0),t).estimate1,r1=dm::stats::pearson_correlation_test(e.col(1),t).estimate1;
    std::print("| {} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} |\n",names[i],r0,dm::stats::pearson_correlation_test(e.col(1),h).estimate1,std::max(std::abs(r0),std::abs(r1)),dm::stats::trustworthiness(input,e,8),dm::stats::continuity(input,e,8));
    auto p=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();p.points_colored(e.col(0),e.col(1),colors,4).title(names[i]).x_label("coordinate 1").y_label("coordinate 2");layout.add(p);
  }
  auto check=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();check.points_colored(h,diffusion.dimension(1),colors,4).title("Height is not coordinate 2").x_label("known height h").y_label("diffusion coordinate 2");layout.add(check);
  layout.size(1100,1000).title("Identical raw input; colour runs from low t (purple) to high t (yellow)").fit_panels().save_svg("build/diffusion_controls.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| method | r(coord1,t) | r(coord2,height) | best abs r(t), first two | trustworthiness k8 | continuity k8 |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Diffusion maps | -0.9375 | -0.0271 | 0.9375 | 0.9030 | 0.9780 |
| PCA unscaled | 0.2426 | 0.0022 | 0.2426 | 0.9057 | 0.9792 |
| Classical MDS | 0.2426 | -0.0022 | 0.2426 | 0.9057 | 0.9792 |
\image(figures/diffusion_controls.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Diffusion maps, unscaled PCA and classical MDS on identical observations, plus the explicit height-recovery failure. All panels use the same angle colour mapping.)
\alttext(Four scatter panels show the three two-coordinate embeddings and known height against the second diffusion coordinate; PCA and MDS have matching geometry up to eigenvector sign.)

The selected diffusion embedding reaches absolute angle correlation 0.9375,
compared with 0.2426 for the first two PCA or MDS coordinates. Its ambient
trustworthiness is 0.9030, versus 0.9057 for both controls; continuity is
0.9780 versus 0.9792. Better angle recovery here accompanies slightly lower
ambient neighbour-rank preservation. The plots and scores answer different
questions about the same sample.

> What these checks establish

The tiny example verifies the actual kernel, row normalization, stationary
weights, spectrum and time-dependent diffusion distance against rational
values. The native tests independently multiply transition matrices and
compare complete spectral distances for 108 seeded point/alpha fits at four
integer times. They also check reversibility, weighted orthogonality, right
eigenpair residuals, fractional-time filtering, exact ordinary-model/trace
agreement, missing-row alignment, owned storage and degenerate duplicate data.
Sorted integer-distance controls exercise every neighbour rank for sizes
2 through 19; centroid controls check the scatter identity and similarity
invariance. The explicit generator is checked against all 400 original
interleaved draws, while its Xoshiro branch matches the original API.

Rows with any selected-feature null are omitted; kept_row_indices() is the
alignment contract for labels or known coordinates. Nonfinite retained
features, invalid schemas, nonfinite/nonpositive epsilon, alpha outside
[0,1] and nonfinite/negative time are rejected. Squared-distance overflow
raises an error. If underflow makes the floating-point affinity graph
disconnected, fitting raises an error rather than discarding an arbitrary
component indicator as the sole constant mode. Connected but nearly
disconnected kernels can still have poorly separated eigenvalues and
unstable eigenvectors. The trace exposes that spectrum; plotting alone
does not diagnose it. Existing constructor names, option field order and
defaults, summary/plot accessors and binding facade signatures are retained.

On iris, alpha affects labelled scatter without making alpha=1 an automatic
winner. On the original roll, the local kernel captures the angular ordering
much better than the broad kernel or linear controls. The weak height
correlations remain an explicit limitation, including all four inspected
coordinates at epsilon=5. Neither a visually ordered curve nor one large
correlation proves a faithful two-dimensional unrolling. Raw feature units,
bandwidth, density weighting, truncation and diffusion time jointly determine
the geometry being shown.

> References

- R. R. Coifman and S. Lafon (2006), “Diffusion maps”, Applied and Computational
  Harmonic Analysis 21(1), 5–30. [Original paper](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.acha.2006.04.006),
  [open manuscript](https://www.math.wustl.edu/~victor/classes/pmf/Lafon06.pdf).
- S. T. Roweis and L. K. Saul (2000), “Nonlinear dimensionality reduction by
  locally linear embedding”, Science 290, 2323–2326.
  [Original paper](https://doi.org/10.1126/science.290.5500.2323).
- J. Venna and S. Kaski (2001), “Neighborhood preservation in nonlinear
  projection methods: An experimental study”, ICANN, 485–491.
  [Original paper](https://doi.org/10.1007/3-540-44668-0_68).
